Yes, I have written Declarative Pipelines. They are defined using the pipeline {} block in a Jenkinsfile. Declarative syntax provides a more structured way of defining the pipeline with stages, steps, and post actions.
How this pulling of your source code can be configured as a part of a Declarative Pipeline?

Answer:
In a Declarative Pipeline, pulling the source code is typically done in the agent block or the stages block:
groovy
Copy code
pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                checkout([$class: 'GitSCM', branches: [[name: '*/main']], userRemoteConfigs: [[url: 'https://github.com/your-repo.git']]])
            }
        }
        // Other stages
    }
}
What is agent any means?

Answer:
agent any specifies that the pipeline can run on any available Jenkins agent/node. This provides flexibility for the job to be executed on any configured agent within Jenkins.
If I want to run my agent in a specific agent, how can I do that?

Answer:
You can specify a particular agent using the label keyword:
groovy
Copy code
pipeline {
    agent {
        label 'specific-agent-label'
    }
    stages {
        // Define stages here
    }
}
How do you use passwords in this Declarative Pipeline?

Answer:
Use Jenkins credentials binding to handle passwords securely:
groovy
Copy code
pipeline {
    agent any
    environment {
        MY_SECRET = credentials('my-secret-id')
    }
    stages {
        stage('Use Secret') {
            steps {
                sh 'echo $MY_SECRET'
            }
        }
    }
}
